{"qid":"Q202266","names":["Old Kingdom of Norway"],"title":"Old Kingdom of Norway","endonym":null,"capsule":"The Kingdom of Norway emerged as a unified polity in the ninth century when Harald Fairhair defeated rival petty kings at the Battle of Hafrsfjord (traditionally c. 872), establishing a hereditary monarchy over the western Norwegian coastline and fjords. The kingdom reached its medieval apogee in the thirteenth century under Haakon IV (r. 1217–1263), who consolidated royal authority, brought Iceland and Greenland under Norwegian sovereignty in 1262–1264, and transformed Bergen into a major commercial center. After the plague devastated Norway's population from 1349, the kingdom entered a long political decline; in 1380 it entered personal union with Denmark under the Oldenburg dynasty, and by the Union of Kalmar (1397) Norway became the junior partner in a Scandinavian union that left it increasingly administered from Copenhagen.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The core Norwegian kingdom on the Scandinavian Peninsula is documented through royal sagas and administrative records. Territorial claims over the Atlantic island dependencies (Iceland, Greenland, Faroe Islands, Orkney, Shetland) are historically attested but their precise cartographic rendering is approximate, particularly for early medieval periods.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Norway","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Norway"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Norway","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norway"}],"britannica":"biography/Margaret-I","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Norway","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Harald I 